# Commits that changed formatting or moved code without changing behaviour.
#
# Enable once, per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# GitHub, GitLab and Azure DevOps read this file automatically.
#
# Only add a PURELY mechanical commit — a re-indent, a rename, a reformat. A commit that also
# changed behaviour must never be listed, or blame points past the change that introduced a
# defect.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  MEASURED: this does NOT rescue a file SPLIT, and the 2026-08-11 split commits are
#  deliberately not listed here.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `--ignore-rev` works by re-attributing a line to the parent commit's version OF THE SAME
# PATH. When a split creates `Foo.Text.cs` out of `Foo.cs`, the new path has no parent
# version, so there is nothing to attribute to and blame stops at the split commit regardless.
#
# `git blame -C -C -C` is the usual advice for this and it was checked here: it does not
# recover the authorship either, on this repository's split commits. Listing them would only
# make the file look like it was doing something.
#
# What actually works for those commits:
#
#   * each split commit's body names the source file, so `git log --follow` on the source and
#     a read of that commit gets you there;
#   * `git log -S'<a line of the code>' -- src/` finds the commit that introduced a line
#     wherever it now lives — this is the reliable one;
#   * the text was moved VERBATIM (no reformatting, no reordering, no using-pruning), so the
#     pre-split history of `StandardEncodings.cs`, `PdfOperations.cs`,
#     `ContentStreamInterpreter.cs`, `PdfSvgRenderer.cs`,
#     `PdfDeepAccessibilityChecker.cs`, `PdfRenderContext.cs`, `HtmlToBuilderMapper.cs` and
#     `PdfFontEmbeddingRemediator.cs` still reads correctly for every line that came out of
#     them.
#
# The file is kept because it is the right place for the next tree-wide reformat, and because
# recording what was tried is cheaper than the next person trying it again.

